{
 "id": "rewrite/fixed-restore-cost/hot/5m",
 "description": "mask compacts a 5,000-token output to 500 (saves 4,500). On request 2 the model expands it, and fixed_restore appends the original as a new 5,000-token message while the marker stays. The restore grows the body, so it is a cost by its position: written once on request 2 (5,000 x $3.75 = -$0.01875) and read on every later hot request (-$0.00150), against the mask's +$0.00135. Each later request nets -$0.00015: the compaction's credit is never shown without the restore beside it.",
 "source": "issue_cases.md#481",
 "dims": {
  "kind": "rewrite",
  "position": "afterBStar",
  "cache": "hit",
  "ttl": "5m",
  "lifetime": "exact",
  "operations": "2",
  "restore": "fixed"
 },
 "rates": "fixture-v1",
 "open": [
  "design \u00a75.1 snaps B* to the last client breakpoint at or before the previous body's end, and the client's breakpoints are the current request's own: system and the new last message. Read literally that gives the end of system, not the end of the previous body. The examples, and this case, take the end of the previous body.",
  "design \u00a76.2 does not say how B* maps onto content inserted after it. The restored message is forwarded messages[4], past client B* (messages[3]), so a literal reading prices it as a write; this case prices it as a read on the later turns because only that conserves"
 ],
